===Etymology 2===
{{etymon|en|id=verb|:der|frm:garder|fro:guarder|tree=1}}
From early {{der|en|frm|garder}} or late {{der|en|fro|-}} (circa 14th cent) {{m|fro|guarder|t=to keep, ward, guard, save, preserve, etc.}}, from {{der|en|la-eme|wardo}}, from {{der|en|frk|*wardēn|}}, from {{der|en|gem-pro|*wardāną|t=to guard, protect}}. Cognate with {{cog|fr|garder}}, {{cog|ang|weardian}} (whence also the English inherited {{glossary|doublet}} {{doublet|en|ward|notext=1}}). See also {{cog|en|regard}}.

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[protect]] from [[danger]]; to [[secure]] against surprise, attack, or injury; to keep in safety; to defend.
#: {{ux|en|All of the [[mountain pass]]es are '''guard'''ed by troops.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Richard 2|III|ii|passage=For Heaven still '''guards''' the right.}}
#* {{RQ:Tennyson In Memoriam|XVII|28|Henceforth, wherever thou may’st roam,<br>⁠My blessing, like a line of light,<br>⁠Is on the waters day and night,<br>And like a beacon '''guards''' thee home.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To keep watch over, in order to prevent escape or restrain from acts of violence, or the like.
#: {{ux|en|'''Guard''' the prisoner.}}
# {{lb|en|ambitransitive}} To watch [[by way of]] caution or defense; to be cautious; to be in a state or position of defense or safety.
#: {{ux|en|Careful people '''guard''' against mistakes.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To protect the edge of, especially with an ornamental border; hence, to face or ornament with lists, laces, etc.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Much Ado About Nothing|I|i|passage=The body of your discourse is sometime '''guarded''' with fragments, and the guards are but slightly basted on neither.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To fasten by [[bind]]ing; to [[gird]].

