===Etymology 1===
{{etymid|en|weather noun}}
From {{inh|en|enm|hayl}}, {{m|enm|haul}}, from {{inh|en|ang|hæġl}}, {{m|ang|hæġel}}, {{m|ang|hagol|t=hail}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*hagl}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*haglaz}}, of {{unc|en|nocap=1}} origin. Either from {{der|en|ine-pro|*kagʰlos|t=pebble}}; or alternatively from {{m|ine-pro|*ḱoḱló-}}, a reduplication of {{m|ine-pro|*ḱel-|t=cold}}. Cognate with {{cog|stq|Hail|t=hail}}, {{cog|fy|heil|t=hail}}, {{cog|nds|Hagel|t=hail}}, {{cog|de|Hagel|t=hail}}, {{cog|nl,sv|hagel|t=hail}}, {{cog|da,nb,nn,is|hagl|t=hail}}. Compare also {{cog|non|héla|t=frost}}. {{doublet|en|haglaz}}, if the second etymology (“cold”) is correct.

Root-cognates outside of Germanic include {{cog|grc|κάχληξ|t=pebble}}, or alternatively Sanskrit {{m|sa|शिशिर|t=cool, cold|tr=śíśira}}, possibly also {{cog|lt|šešėlis|t=shade, shadow}}, depending on the etymology.

====Noun====
[[Image:Hand holding hail in a strawberry patch.jpg|thumb]]
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|meteorology|uncountable}} {{cap|ball}}s or [[piece]]s of [[ice]] falling as [[precipitation]], often in connection with a [[thunderstorm]].
# {{lb|en|meteorology|countable}} An [[occurrence]] of this type of precipitation; a [[hailstorm]].
# {{lb|en|countable|by extension}} A [[rapid]], [[intense]] [[barrage]] by a large number of [[projectile]]s or other objects.
#: {{syn|en|downpouring}}
#* {{quote-av|en|author=Drachinifel|title=The Battle of Samar - Odds? What are those?|date=27 February 2019|url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4AdcvDiA3lE|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20221103043318/https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4AdcvDiA3lE|archivedate=3 November 2022|accessdate=5 November 2022|time=40:01|text=Their lack of good intelligence also meant that they vastly overestimated the size of their foes for far too long, '''hails''' of armor-piercing shells doing comparatively little damage compared to the high explosive that they ''should'' have been using.}}

