# A [[ditch]] along the side of a [[road]].
#* {{quote-book|en|passage=In nearly all of the towns the '''gutters''' are filled with vegetation, or have been neglected for so long a time that the roadway becomes its own drainage bed.|title=Annual Report of the Massachusetts Highway Commission|volume=9|author={{w|Massachusetts Highway Department|Massachusetts Highway Commission}}|year=1902}}
#* {{RQ:Allingham China Governess|chapter=7|passage=‘Children crawled over each other like little grey worms in the '''gutters''',’ he said. ‘The only red things about them were their buttocks and they were raw. Their faces looked as if snails had slimed on them and their mothers were like great sick beasts whose byres had never been cleared.{{nb...}}’}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Trophy Boy|page=122|url=https://books.google.com/books?isbn=1425909078|author=Henry Clark|year=2006|passage=As Mike parked the vehicle, its right wheels sank into an unpaved '''gutter''' gradually worn irregular and deep by the rush of rainwater flowing down the street.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=w:A Thousand Splendid Suns|url=https://books.google.com/books?isbn=1101010908|author=w:Khaled Hosseini|year=2008|passage='''Gutters''' separated the sidewalk from the road on both sides and flowed with muddy water.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Murphy's Child|url=https://books.google.com/books?isbn=1459258991|author=Judith Duncan|year=2011|passage=Meltwater gathered in the icy ruts of the unpaved road, the pressure wearing thin channels in the packed snow. Along the '''gutter''' the rivulets of spring runoff cut a course to the storm sewer}}
# A [[duct]] or [[channel]] beneath the [[eave]]s of a [[building]] to carry [[rain]] [[water]]; [[eavestrough]].
#: {{ux|en|The '''gutters''' must be cleared of leaves a few times a year.}}
# {{lb|en|bowling}} A [[groove]] down the [[side]]s of a [[bowling]] [[lane]].
#: {{ux|en|You can decide to use the bumpers to avoid the ball going down the '''gutter''' every time.}}
# A large groove (commonly behind animals) in a barn used for the collection and removal of animal excrement.
# Any narrow channel or groove, such as one formed by erosion in the vent of a gun from repeated firing.
# {{lb|en|typography}} A space between printed columns of text.
# {{lb|en|printing}} One of a number of pieces of wood or metal, grooved in the centre, used to separate the pages of type in a [[form]].
# {{lb|en|philately}} An [[unprinted]] space between rows of [[stamp]]s.
# {{lb|en|British}} A [[drainage]] [[channel]].
# The notional locus of things, acts, or events that are distasteful, ill-bred, or morally questionable.
#* {{quote-av|en|author=Julian Schnabel|authorlink=Julian Schnabel|actor={{w|Elina Löwensohn}}|role={{w|Annina Nosei}}|title=[[w:Basquiat (film)|Basquiat]]|location=United States|publisher=Eleventh Street Productions; distributed by {{w|Miramax Films}}|year=1996|text=Nosei (Löwensohn): This is Tom and Cynthia Kruger. / {{w|Jean-Michel Basquiat}} ({{w|Jeffrey Wright}}): I heard. / Nosei: This is Jean-Michel Basquiat. / Tom Kruger (Chuck Pfeiffer): Hi. / Nosei: You've seen the SAMO graffiti everywhere&nbsp;– that's his. This is the true voice of the '''gutter'''.}}
# {{lb|en|figuratively}} A low, vulgar state.
#: {{ux|en|[[get one's mind out of the gutter|Get your mind out of the '''gutter''']].}}
#: {{ux|en|What kind of '''gutter''' language is that? I ought to wash your mouth out with soap.}}
#* {{RQ:Wilde Lady Windermere|act=III|passage={{smallcaps|Lord Darlington.}} No, we are all in the '''gutter''', but some of us are looking at the stars.}}
