====Usage notes====
The term {{m|en|gravity wave}} is sometimes used as a synonym for {{m|en||gravitational wave}}, and vice versa. However, ''gravity wave'' generally refers to the waves formed where two fluids meet under gravity, such as wind waves on the ocean surface, and use of the term to describe the astrophysical phenomenon is proscribed.<ref>{{cite-web|author=Ian O'Neill|title=Gravitational Waves vs. Gravity Waves: Know the Difference!|url=http://news.discovery.com/space/gravitational-waves-vs-gravity-waves-know-the-difference-160208.htm|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20160210091412/http://news.discovery.com/space/gravitational-waves-vs-gravity-waves-know-the-difference-160208.htm|archivedate=10 February 2016|work={{w|Discovery Channel}}|date=8 February 2016}}.</ref>

